{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbHsaxfrQfr4rqQYqHHi", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The main difference is that a list is mutable and a tuple is immutable.\n\n| | **List** | **Tuple** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` |\n| Mutability | Can add, remove, or change items | Cannot change after creation |\n| Hashable | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member | Yes, if all its items are hashable |\n| Methods | Many: `append`, `extend`, `pop`, `sort`, and so on | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Memory and speed | Slightly more overhead | Slightly smaller and faster |\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums[0] = 99          # fine\nnums.append(4)        # fine\n\npoint = (1, 2)\npoint[0] = 99         # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\n**When to use which**\n- **List:** Use it for a collection that changes or grows, and whose items are all the same kind of thing, such as a list of usernames.\n- **Tuple:** Use it for a fixed record whose positions have meaning, such as `(x, y)`, `(name, age)`, or a function returning multiple values. Use it when you need a dict key too, such as `{(0, 0): \"origin\"}`.\n\n**Caveats**\n- A one-element tuple needs a trailing comma: `(5,)`. Without it, `(5)` is just the integer 5.\n- Tuples are only shallowly immutable. If a tuple contains a list, that list can still be modified: `t = ([1], 2); t[0].append(3)` works."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 6678, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 6678}, "output_tokens": 3, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "292fce4c-a